<p>AM4 is still one of the most common gaming platforms, and it has one big advantage: you can upgrade the processor without changing the motherboard or DDR4 memory. But which AM4 CPU is actually the best for gaming in 2026, and is the jump from a Ryzen 5 5600 to a 5700X3D or 5800X3D worth it?</p>
<p>We compared all 7 AM4 processors in our database using the gaming score and engine behind our <a href="/pc-bottleneck-calculator/">PC Bottleneck Calculator</a> and <a href="/cpu-tier-list/">CPU tier list</a>. These are estimates from our gaming-performance scores (placed from published review averages), not our own benchmarks.</p>

<h2>Quick answer</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Best AM4 CPU for gaming:</strong> the <a href="/hardware/ryzen-7-5800x3d/">Ryzen 7 5800X3D</a> (gaming score 70), thanks to its 3D V-Cache. It is 35% ahead of the Ryzen 5 5600 in our index.</li>
<li><strong>Close second:</strong> the <a href="/hardware/ryzen-7-5700x3d/">Ryzen 7 5700X3D</a> (score 66, 27% ahead of the 5600), the same 8-core 3D V-Cache design at lower clocks (4.1 vs 4.5 GHz boost).</li>
<li><strong>Budget pick:</strong> the <a href="/hardware/ryzen-5-5600/">Ryzen 5 5600</a> (score 52, 88W) is enough for cards up to the RTX 5060 Ti 16GB at 1080p and the RX 7900 XT at 1440p without a noticeable bottleneck.</li>
<li><strong>Skip:</strong> the Ryzen 7 5800X and 5700X add 2 cores over the 5600 but only 5 and 3 points of gaming score; for gaming, the X3D chips are the meaningful step up.</li>
</ul>

