<p>With an RTX 5070 at 1080p, the 5600 leaves about 32% of the card idle; the 5700X3D cuts that to 14% and the 5800X3D to 8%. At 1440p and 4K the graphics card is the limit with all three, so if you play at 1440p or higher on an RTX 5060 Ti-class card, the 5600 is still enough and the money is better spent on the GPU. Head-to-head pages: <a href="/comparisons/ryzen-5-5600-vs-ryzen-7-5700x3d/">Ryzen 5 5600 vs 5700X3D</a> and <a href="/comparisons/ryzen-7-5700x3d-vs-ryzen-7-5800x3d/">5700X3D vs 5800X3D</a>.</p>
<p>Before swapping the CPU, check your motherboard maker's CPU support list and update the BIOS, and note that the X3D chips are rated at 105W TDP (142W maximum) against the 5600's 65W, so check your cooler too. Our <a href="/pc-upgrade-calculator/">PC Upgrade Calculator</a> shows whether the CPU or the graphics card is the better next upgrade for your build.</p>

<h2>AM4 or move to AM5?</h2>
<p>In our index the Ryzen 7 5700X3D (66) is level with the <a href="/hardware/ryzen-5-7600/">Ryzen 5 7600</a> (67), and the 5800X3D (70) is a little ahead of it. Moving to AM5 means a new motherboard and DDR5 memory as well as the CPU, so for a gaming-only upgrade on an existing AM4 system, an X3D chip gets you similar gaming performance for just the cost of the processor. AM5 makes more sense for a new build, or if you want the headroom of a <a href="/hardware/ryzen-7-7800x3d/">Ryzen 7 7800X3D</a> (score 87) for a high-end graphics card.</p>

[cs_faq]
[cs_faq_item q="What is the best AM4 CPU for gaming?"]The Ryzen 7 5800X3D, with a gaming score of 70 in our index, followed closely by the Ryzen 7 5700X3D (66). Both use AMD's 3D V-Cache, which is what puts them ahead of the 5800X (57) and 5700X (55).[/cs_faq_item]
[cs_faq_item q="Is the Ryzen 7 5700X3D worth it over the Ryzen 5 5600?"]For gaming at 1080p with a mid-range or faster card, yes: it is 27% ahead in our index and cuts the RTX 5070 bottleneck at 1080p from 32% to 14%. At 1440p and above with a mid-range card, the 5600 is usually still enough.[/cs_faq_item]
[cs_faq_item q="5700X3D or 5800X3D: which should I buy?"]The 5800X3D is about 6% faster in our index because of its higher clocks; both have 8 cores, 16 threads and the same 142W maximum power. If the price gap is large, the 5700X3D gives most of the gain.[/cs_faq_item]
[cs_faq_item q="Is AM4 still worth it in 2026?"]For upgrading an existing AM4 system, yes: an X3D chip matches a Ryzen 5 7600-class AM5 CPU in our index without a new motherboard or DDR5. For a brand-new build, AM5 gives a longer upgrade path.[/cs_faq_item]
[/cs_faq]

